Qt module:   qtmultimedia
Base tag:    v6.11.1
Applies to:  src/multimedia/configure.cmake,
             src/plugins/multimedia/ffmpeg/CMakeLists.txt,
             src/plugins/multimedia/ffmpeg/playbackengine/qffmpegdeinterlacer.cpp (new),
             src/plugins/multimedia/ffmpeg/playbackengine/qffmpegdeinterlacer_p.h (new),
             src/plugins/multimedia/ffmpeg/playbackengine/qffmpegvideorenderer.cpp,
             src/plugins/multimedia/ffmpeg/playbackengine/qffmpegvideorenderer_p.h

Adds optional software deinterlacing (yadif/bwdif) for the FFmpeg playback
path, since Qt Multimedia has neither an avfilter/filter-graph stage nor
any interlace awareness at all: StreamDecoder::receiveAVFrames()
(qffmpegstreamdecoder.cpp) hands avcodec_receive_frame()'s raw AVFrame
straight into a Frame/QVideoFrame, discarding AV_FRAME_FLAG_INTERLACED /
top_field_first entirely, and no public API (QMediaPlayer, QVideoSink,
QVideoFrame, QPlaybackOptions) exposes anything deinterlace-related
either. This is the same class of gap as the AV1-hwaccel and WSOLA
pitch-compensation patches -- Qt Multimedia gives Vivace nothing to call,
so the fix has to live inside qtmultimedia itself.

Per this project's working rule ("read the reference implementation
before implementing a feature"), SMPlayer's own Video > Deinterlace menu
was read first (src/basegui.cpp, src/mediasettings.h, src/prefgeneral.cpp/
preftv.cpp): a radio-button submenu with modes None / Lowpass5 / Yadif
(normal) / Yadif (double framerate) / Linear Blend / Kerndeint, backed by
a two-level model (Preferences::initial_deinterlace, a global default,
seeds MediaSettings::current_deinterlacer, a per-file/session value).
SMPlayer applies these via mplayer -vf-add / mpv's lavfi(); Lowpass5 and
Linear Blend map to libpostproc's `pp=l5`/`pp=lb`, Kerndeint to its own
`kerndeint` filter.

Deliberately scoped DOWN from SMPlayer's six modes to two -- Yadif and
Bwdif only -- for two reasons, not laziness:

  1. Lowpass5/Linear Blend/Kerndeint depend on libpostproc, a separate,
     older FFmpeg library that may not even be present in the dav1d-
     enabled BtbN FFmpeg build Vivace's other patches already depend on
     (see scripts/build-patched-qtmultimedia-{linux,windows}.*) --
     claiming modes that might silently be unavailable would be the kind
     of dishonest-scope call this project has avoided elsewhere (e.g. the
     file-association extension list only advertises formats Vivace can
     actually play).
  2. The "double framerate" (bob) variants need the ability to emit an
     EXTRA, synthesized output frame with its own timestamp per input
     frame -- but QFFmpeg::Renderer's frame-queueing (qffmpegrenderer_p.h)
     is a private, one-Frame-in-per-renderInternal()-call contract with no
     way for a subclass to inject a second frame into the session queue.
     Supporting bob mode would need much deeper surgery to Renderer's own
     scheduling, out of scope for this patch. Yadif/Bwdif "mode=send_frame"
     (one output per input) fits VideoRenderer::renderInternal()'s existing
     one-in/one-out contract exactly, with no restructuring needed.

Verified all AVOption names/defaults and the shared "1-frame-lookahead
latency" behaviour directly against FFmpeg's real n7.1 source (not
recalled from memory): yadif.h (YADIF_MODE_SEND_FRAME=0, YADIF_PARITY_
AUTO=-1, YADIF_DEINT_ALL=0), yadif_common.c (both the mid-stream and
end-of-stream frame-emission paths call av_frame_copy_props(), so
colour-space/HDR/side-data metadata already carries through to the
deinterlaced output frame with no manual propagation needed), and
vf_bwdif.c (confirms bwdif shares yadif's exact option names via the same
yadif_common.c infrastructure, but its own MODE DEFAULT is
YADIF_MODE_SEND_FIELD=1/"double framerate" -- this patch always passes
mode=0 explicitly to force the single-rate case regardless of that
default). The buffer-source args-string format
("video_size=%1x%2:pix_fmt=%3:time_base=...:pixel_aspect=%4/%5", with
pix_fmt as a plain integer) and the av_buffersrc_add_frame_flags(...,
AV_BUFFERSRC_FLAG_KEEP_REF) / av_buffersink_get_frame(...) push/pull calls
match FFmpeg's own official doc/examples/decode_filter_video.c reference
example line for line.

Design (all in the new Deinterlacer class, qffmpegdeinterlacer_p.h/.cpp):
  - A small 3-node avfilter graph (buffer -> yadif|bwdif -> buffersink),
    rebuilt lazily whenever the input frame's width/height/pixel format
    changes (or on first use). The buffersink's accepted pix_fmts is
    constrained to match the input, so the pass-through filter can't
    trigger an unwanted implicit conversion.
  - Since this class substitutes its OWN external timing for each frame
    rather than trusting the filter graph's own pts math, a plain FIFO
    (QQueue<pair<TrackPosition, TrackDuration>>) pairs "the Nth frame
    pushed" with "the Nth frame emitted" -- valid because yadif/bwdif in
    mode=send_frame never reorder frames, only delay them by a fixed,
    constant one-frame lookahead.
  - Hardware-decoded frames (inputFrame->hw_frames_ctx set -- the DEFAULT
    path for exactly the codecs, H.264/MPEG-2, that interlaced content is
    most likely to use) are downloaded to system memory via
    av_hwframe_transfer_data() before entering the graph, since yadif/
    bwdif are software-only filters; skipping this would make
    deinterlacing silently do nothing (or fail) on the common case.
  - VideoRenderer::renderInternal() (the sole AVFrame -> QVideoFrame
    conversion point in the whole FFmpeg plugin) is gated by a
    VIVACE_DEINTERLACE_MODE environment variable (0 = off, 1 = Yadif,
    2 = Bwdif, 3 = Auto -- see below), read fresh via
    qEnvironmentVariableIntValue() on every frame -- the same
    env-var-relay pattern already used by VIVACE_SUBTITLE_BITMAP_SMOOTHING,
    since this plugin has no reach into Vivace's own Settings/
    PlayerController. Vivace's PlayerController::setDeinterlaceMode()
    calls qputenv() on every change.
  - A new VideoRenderer::seekInternal() override drops any buffered
    Deinterlacer state on seek (it refers to pre-seek frames and would
    otherwise pair stale timing with a post-seek frame); the same reset
    happens at end-of-stream.
  - CMakeLists.txt/configure.cmake changes just add AVFILTER to the
    qt_find_package(FFmpeg ...) component list and link FFmpeg::avfilter
    into the plugin -- both scripts/build-patched-qtmultimedia-*.* already
    download a "gpl-shared" BtbN FFmpeg build, which includes libavfilter;
    they were updated to also copy its runtime library alongside the
    others.

KNOWN LIMITATION (documented in qffmpegdeinterlacer_p.h): because of the
one-frame lookahead latency, the very last frame pushed before end of
stream is never retrieved (nothing pushes a following frame to trigger
it, and this class does not flush the graph at EOF) -- a single dropped
frame at the very end of deinterlaced playback. Considered not worth the
extra complexity of an EOF-flush path for one frame.

BUILD FIX (found via a REAL rebuild attempt, media/qtmm-build-log.txt --
the first real evidence this patch was ever actually compiled): MSVC
rejected the original version with C2280 "attempting to reference a
deleted function" for both `TrackPosition outStartTime;` and
`TrackDuration outDuration;` in qffmpegvideorenderer.cpp. Root cause,
confirmed by reading qtaggedtime_p.h directly: `TrackPosition`/
`TrackDuration` (QTaggedTimePoint/QTaggedDuration<qint64, TrackTimeTag>)
derive from `BaseTime<...>`, which defines ONLY a constructor taking an
explicit `ValueType` (qint64) plus a copy constructor -- no default
(no-argument) constructor at all, so declaring either type with no
initializer fails to compile. Fixed by seeding both with an explicit `0`
(`TrackPosition outStartTime(0); TrackDuration outDuration(0);`) --
`Deinterlacer::process()` overwrites both before they're used whenever it
returns true, so the seed value itself is never actually read. Checked
the new qffmpegdeinterlacer.cpp/.h file for the same mistake: no bare
TrackPosition/TrackDuration is ever default-constructed there (the
QQueue<pair<...>> member itself doesn't require its value type to be
default-constructible; every actual TrackPosition/TrackDuration in that
file is either a copy, an aggregate-initialized pair element, or an
assignment through a reference parameter -- none of which need a default
constructor), so no other fix was needed. Re-verified with the SAME
method as before this fix (git apply --check + git apply + byte-diff, on
a fresh `git clone --branch v6.11.1`, both alone and stacked with all
three other patches) after applying this correction.

