PER-SINK OPT-OUT ADDED 2026-08-27 (real regression found the day after
the swap above: "The movie thumbnail is now displaying black box only"
-- the seek-bar hover-preview thumbnail, not the (unrelated, external-
ffmpeg-process-based) playlist row thumbnail cache). Root-caused
directly: Vivace has a SEPARATE, hidden, paused `QMediaPlayer` for the
seek-bar hover preview (`PlayerController::m_previewPlayer`/
`m_previewSink`) -- it does exactly one `setPosition()` per hover and
expects exactly one resulting frame, with nothing further arriving
after it. yadif/bwdif's own one-frame lookahead (see this patch's
original header above) means `Deinterlacer::process()` can NEVER
produce output for a frame that's the only one it will ever receive --
`VideoRenderer::renderInternal()` never calls `setVideoFrame()` for it,
so the preview sink just stays empty (solid black). This bug existed
latently since deinterlacing first shipped (anyone who manually chose
Yadif/Bwdif would have hit it too), but Auto becoming the DEFAULT the
day before turned it from "a rare, never-reported edge case" into
"every user hits it on every hover."
Considered and REJECTED a fix inside `Deinterlacer` itself (push the
stuck frame's own data a second time to force yadif/bwdif to emit
something immediately): traced through the filter graph's own internal
FIFO state carefully and found it would leave a stray, already-primed
"extra" frame sitting in the graph after the fallback fires -- the
NEXT genuinely new frame to arrive (in an ordinary CONTINUOUS playback
session that happens to hit this same first-frame case, e.g. right
after any mode change or seek) would then incorrectly drain THAT
stray frame's own output instead of its own, corrupting frame content/
timing for one frame every time the graph is rebuilt -- a worse,
harder-to-notice bug than the one being fixed. A timer-based flush
(wait briefly for a second frame; only fall back once none arrives)
would be more correct but requires real confidence in this private
Renderer's own threading model to implement and test safely, not
attempted under time pressure for a live regression.
INSTEAD (the user's own suggestion, confirmed to be exactly right):
since Vivace's own `PlayerController` constructs BOTH `QVideoSink`
instances itself (the main one via QML's `VideoOutput`, and
`m_previewSink` directly in C++), and `QVideoSink` is an ordinary
`QObject`, `VideoRenderer::renderInternal()` now checks a plain
dynamic property (`m_sink->property("vivace_no_deinterlace")`)
alongside the existing `VIVACE_DEINTERLACE_MODE` env-var check --
`PlayerController` sets this property to `true` on `m_previewSink`
once, right after constructing it (Vivace-side change, not part of
this patch). This completely bypasses the Deinterlacer for that ONE
sink regardless of the global mode, while leaving the main player's
own sink -- and therefore ordinary continuous playback, including
Auto-by-default -- entirely untouched. Zero risk to the Deinterlacer's
own internal FIFO/timing correctness, since its code isn't touched at
all; a tiny, briefly-shown hover thumbnail was never going to show a
visible deinterlacing difference anyway, so skipping it there is a
correct trade, not just a convenient one.
